{
  "image_name": "0b9460262dea2e2b59fbab4e8e22fa2e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes strategies for protein semisynthesis using split inteins, either alone or combined with sortase. Overall, it shows how a protein of interest can be selectively modified at the N terminus, C terminus, or an internal site by bringing together intein fragments and then triggering protein trans-splicing (PTS).\n\nThe top part illustrates three general layouts. For N-terminal or C-terminal modification, an intein fragment is attached to the target protein and paired with the complementary fragment carrying the desired tag or peptide. After intein fragment association, splicing links the new piece to the protein and releases the intein fragments. A central modification scheme extends this idea to insert a segment within the middle of a protein, producing two modified protein fragments plus excised intein pieces.\n\nThe lower part adds sortase A29 as a preparative step for generating suitable intein-bearing intermediates. Sortase-mediated transpeptidation creates truncated N-tail or C-tail constructs with short recognition motifs, which then associate with complementary intein fragments and undergo PTS to yield the final labeled or remodeled protein products. The main message is a modular workflow for site-specific protein editing at multiple positions.",
